" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "30 July –  3 August 1996"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/718"} "Lake Sidney Lanier, Gainesville, Georgia"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/256080"} "23 from 23 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10397"} "Knut Holmann"] " of Norway was the defending Olympic runner-up and winner of the K-1 1000 at two of the last three editions of the World Championships, making him a favorite for the event at the 1996 Summer Olympics. His greatest challenger was likely to be Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9033"} "Clint Robinson"] ", the reigning Olympic and 1994 World Champion. Holmann won his heat in the opening round, but it was Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10159"} "Beniamino Bonomi"] " who posted the fastest time, defeating Robinson in the process. Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9543"} "Mikko Kolehmainen"] ", the reigning K-1 500 Olympic champion, took a third heat. Bonomi then bested Holmann in the semi-finals, while Kolehmainen was fourth in Robinson’s heat. In the final, however, the Norwegian got the best of the Italian and finished over a second ahead and in Olympic record time to win the gold medal, while Bonomi settled for silver nearly three seconds ahead of Robinson. The Australian had to fend off a challenge from Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9949"} "Lutz Liwowski"] " to earn bronze, which he did by a margin of 0.312 seconds. Holmann would go on to take silver in the K-1 500, while Bonomi would be runner-up again in the K-2 500."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "220"] [:td 